        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a sugary nutrition drink by default</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some classic older-adult nutrition drinks carry around 15 g of sugar or more per bottle and only about 9 to 10 g of protein. That suits someone who needs calories above all, but for most seniors the goal is protein. Read the label and look for about 25 to 30 g of protein with 2 g of sugar or less.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting extra protein with kidney disease</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Higher protein targets are for people with healthy kidneys. Adults with stage 3 to 5 chronic kidney disease who are not on dialysis are usually advised to eat less protein, not more. Ask about kidney function on a recent blood test before adding a daily shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Splitting protein into small doses</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Older muscle needs a bigger signal. Sipping 10 g of protein at a time through the day does less than a single 25 to 30 g serving. If appetite is the barrier, choose a dense ready-to-drink bottle rather than a large, thin shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping strength exercise</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein on its own does relatively little for muscle in older adults. Resistance exercise, even chair stands and bands two or three times a week, is what makes the protein count. A physical therapist can set up a safe routine for someone with joint pain or a history of falls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring medication interactions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many nutrition shakes are fortified with vitamin K, potassium and phosphorus. Vitamin K affects warfarin, and potassium and phosphorus matter for kidney disease. Show the label to a pharmacist or doctor if you take several medications, and count the shake if you are on a fluid restriction.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

